"""Reader reference material and assessment, distinct from planned chapters."""
EXERCISES = [
('conceptual','C1','A program adds a task to a dictionary and displays “Saved.” What can it do while running, and what happens when it starts with a new dictionary?',
 'While the program runs, it can list and update the task in that dictionary. After restart, a new empty dictionary contains none of the previous tasks. To keep the task across a restart, the program must save it in persistent storage and wait for the save to finish before reporting success.'),
('conceptual','C2','What does the signature create(title) tell a caller? What must the contract explain?',
 'The signature names the operation and its input; a typed signature can also describe the result type. The contract explains the behaviour: which titles are valid, what changes when the call succeeds, and which errors may occur. In our persistent application, it also says that creation returns successfully only after commit. Callers need these rules to use the operation correctly.'),
('conceptual','C3','The task service depends on a repository. Does this dependency necessarily make the design difficult to change?',
 'No. The service needs a way to save tasks. A repository contract lets it request that work without knowing the SQL or file layout. A column rename can then be handled in the repository. The useful question is how much one module must know about another and whether a change to those details spreads through the application.'),
('conceptual','C4','How do a high-level view, a low-level view, and a deployment view help explain the task application?',
 'The high-level view shows the main parts, such as the application and database. The low-level view explains the service methods, task records, and repository interface inside the application. The deployment view places the running application and database file on machines. These views describe the same system, so their decisions must agree. A separate repository box in a module diagram does not imply a separate server.'),
('conceptual','C5','A service stops accepting requests during recovery but keeps every committed task. Which observation concerns availability, and which concerns durability?',
 'The period when users cannot perform task operations concerns availability. The fact that committed tasks remain concerns durability under that recovery scenario. A system can preserve data while being temporarily unavailable. Conversely, it can keep replying while losing tasks that were stored only in memory.'),
('scenario','S1','Task 7 is committed, but its success reply is lost. The client waits, then retries. Can it conclude that the first attempt failed?',
 'No. In this sequence, task 7 exists even though the client has not received the reply. A fresh create request may add task 8. To recognise the retry, the client needs to reuse an operation identifier, and the server must keep that identifier with the committed result. The design must also specify how it checks that the repeated request matches and how long it remembers the result. Chapter 8.2 develops these rules.'),
('scenario','S2','The web adapter and command-line adapter each enforce an 80-character title limit. Only the web adapter is changed to allow 200 characters. How would you prevent this disagreement?',
 'Move the shared title rule into the task service and make both adapters call it. Each adapter still checks its own input format, such as whether a web request contains valid JSON. Check that the database can store the longer title and update any matching storage constraint. Editing both copied rules fixes today’s mismatch but leaves the same risk for the next change.'),
('scenario','S3','The team runs two application machines, each with its own unrelated local task database. A user sees a task on one request and cannot find it on the next. What could explain this?',
 'The two requests may have reached different machines. A task saved in one local database is absent from the other. The design now needs a way for the applications to use shared or coordinated data. Adding a load balancer distributes requests; it does not copy or merge the task databases.'),
('design','D1','A handler adds a task to memory and replies “Saved.” A background writer saves it later. Trace a crash that loses the task, then propose a change that meets the restart requirement.',
 'The failing sequence is: add to memory, reply “Saved,” crash before the background write commits. After restart, memory is empty and there is no saved task to reload. Change the order so that success follows a durable commit, and report a write error if the save fails. A local database is enough for our stated single-machine example. A background queue would need its own durable-save and acknowledgment rules before it could support the same promise.'),
('implementation','I1','Run examples/todo.py. Make a test repository raise a storage error. Then use the real repository to save a task, close it, and read the task through a new connection. What should each check show?',
 'When insert raises a storage error, create must pass that error to its caller rather than return a successful Task. With the real SQLite repository, the task should still be present after closing and reopening the same database. Also submit an invalid title and check that no row is added. The first check covers error handling, the second persistence across reopen, and the third validation. An in-memory substitute cannot establish the persistence result.'),
('thought_experiment','T1','The team now requires saved tasks to survive destruction of the only disk. Why is ADR 001 insufficient, and what must the next design address?',
 'ADR 001 assumes that committed storage remains intact. Destroying its only disk removes the copy it relies on. The new design needs recoverable data on independent storage and a rule for when it may report success. If copying happens only later, a task reported as saved can be lost before the copy is made. Specify which failures the copies must survive, how much loss is acceptable, and how recovery will work. Then test those cases. Simply counting replicas does not answer these questions.')
]

# term, meaning, target; entries are definitions, not claims that later chapters exist.
GLOSSARY = [
('Abstraction','Presenting the operations a caller needs while hiding implementation details; the contract still states the behaviour the caller relies on.','ch-1-2'),
('ADR','Architecture Decision Record: a short record of a design choice, why it was made, its consequences, and when to reconsider it.','ch-1-3'),
('Application architecture','The organization and interaction of application responsibilities and their dependency rules.','unit-7'),
('Availability','Whether a system can provide the required service when needed. A measurement must define which requests and results count.','ch-1-3'),
('Backpressure','A mechanism that communicates or enforces downstream capacity limits on upstream work.','unit-4'),
('Boundary','A line that separates the parts being considered from their surroundings. A diagram should explain whether it marks a system, process, machine, or trust boundary.','ch-1-1'),
('CAP','Under a network partition, the theorem rules out simultaneously guaranteeing its formal consistency and availability properties. It is not a general “pick any two” menu.','unit-14'),
('Cohesion','How closely the work inside a module belongs together.','ch-1-2'),
('Component','A part of a system with a defined responsibility. Several components may run inside one process.','ch-1-1'),
('Composition root','The startup code that creates the objects an application needs and passes them to the parts that use them.','ch-1-2'),
('Concurrency','Multiple activities in progress with potentially interleaved execution; parallel execution is not required.','unit-4'),
('Consistency model','A contract specifying which observations of shared or replicated state are allowed.','unit-14'),
('Container (C4)','An application or data store in a C4 diagram. It need not be a Docker or other operating-system container.','ch-1-4'),
('Contract','The rules for how an operation behaves, including valid inputs, results, state changes, and errors.','ch-1-1'),
('Coupling','The dependencies between parts of a system: what one part must know about or rely on in another.','ch-1-2'),
('Dependency injection','Supplying collaborators to an object or function rather than having it locate or construct them internally.','unit-6'),
('Durability','Whether committed data survives the specified failures, such as a process restart or loss of a disk.','ch-1-3'),
('Encapsulation','Controlling access to internal data so callers use the operations that protect its rules.','ch-1-2'),
('Error budget','The allowed unreliability implied by a chosen SLO over its measurement window.','unit-2'),
('Eventual consistency','A convergence property under the model’s assumptions; it alone gives neither an immediate freshness bound nor a complete conflict policy.','unit-14'),
('Exactly-once processing','A scoped guarantee about effects or records within a defined system boundary and assumptions. It does not automatically include arbitrary external side effects.','unit-16'),
('Failure domain','Elements exposed to a common cause of failure, such as a process, host, disk, or region.','unit-17'),
('HLD','High-Level Design: a view of a system’s main parts, where data is kept, and how the parts work together.','ch-1-1'),
('Idempotency','Repeated application of an operation has the same relevant effect as one application, under its defined identity and scope.','unit-8'),
('Interface','The operations a component offers to its callers. Its contract explains how those operations behave.','ch-1-1'),
('Invariant','A rule that must hold whenever the system is in a valid state, such as unique task identifiers.','ch-1-1'),
('Latency','Elapsed time for an operation under a specified measurement boundary.','unit-2'),
('Linearizability','A correctness condition in which operations appear atomic and respect real-time precedence.','unit-14'),
('LLD','Low-Level Design: the internal detail of a component, including its functions, classes, interfaces, and algorithms.','ch-1-1'),
('Modularity','Organising a system into parts with clear responsibilities and interfaces.','ch-1-2'),
('MVCC','Multiversion concurrency control: retaining versions to help coordinate concurrent database access. Exact visibility and isolation depend on implementation.','unit-10'),
('Nonfunctional requirement','A requirement about the quality or conditions of a system’s behaviour, such as response time or persistence.','ch-1-3'),
('Quorum','A set of participants whose responses meet a protocol’s decision rule. Intersection alone does not establish all read or write correctness properties.','unit-15'),
('Repository','A component that offers operations for storing and retrieving application data. Its contract must explain when changes are committed.','ch-1-2'),
('RPO','Recovery Point Objective: an objective for the amount of data loss measured in time.','unit-17'),
('RTO','Recovery Time Objective: an objective for restoring service after disruption.','unit-17'),
('Saga','A coordinated sequence of local transactions with compensating actions; compensation need not be a perfect inverse.','unit-16'),
('Serializability','A transactional correctness condition requiring equivalence to some serial execution; real-time order is a separate issue.','unit-10'),
('SLI / SLO / SLA','Indicator: measured behavior. Objective: target for that behavior. Agreement: an explicit service agreement, potentially with consequences.','unit-2'),
('State','Information currently held by a system that affects what it will do next.','ch-1-1'),
('Throughput','Completed work per unit time under a specified workload and success definition.','unit-2'),
('Trade-off','A choice that accepts one cost or limitation to gain another benefit, judged against the requirements.','ch-1-3'),
('Transactional outbox','Recording a state change and publication intent in one local transaction, then relaying the intent; duplicate delivery still requires handling.','unit-16'),
('Trust boundary','A point across which identity, privilege, or confidence in input changes.','unit-18'),
]

FORMULAS = [
('State transition','T(S, c) → (S′, result)','Identify valid state changes and preserved invariants. This abstracts away implementation and failures.','ch-1-1','introduced'),
('Weighted preference','score(a) = Σ wᵢ sᵢ(a) / Σ wᵢ','Use nonnegative weights with positive total. Compare only feasible designs. Scores are illustrative judgments, not benchmark results.','ch-1-3','introduced'),
('Request rate','λ = daily users × requests per user per day / 86,400','An average workload estimate. Peak factors must be assumed or measured separately.','unit-2','planned'),
('Little’s Law','L = λ W','Long-run stable averages with consistent boundaries and units. It is not a universal p99 prediction.','unit-2','planned'),
('Amdahl’s Law','speedup(N) = 1 / [(1 − p) + p/N]','Idealized fixed workload and parallel fraction p; excludes extra coordination overhead unless modeled.','unit-2','planned'),
('Storage growth','bytes/day = records/day × bytes/record × retained copies','Account separately for indexes, compression, retention, metadata, and headroom.','unit-2','planned'),
('Error allowance','allowed bad events = (1 − target fraction) × eligible events','Valid for an event-based SLO; time-based objectives need their own denominator.','unit-19','planned'),
('Token refill','tokens′ = min(capacity, tokens + refill_rate × elapsed)','Admission consumes tokens atomically. Distributed clocks and state ownership need additional design.','ch-22-2','planned'),
('Quorum intersection','R + W > N','An intersection condition for reads and writes on a fixed replica set. Not by itself a proof of linearizability.','unit-15','planned'),
('Cost per outcome','cost / successful business outcomes','Define accounting period and include capacity, storage, networking, telemetry, and operational work where relevant.','unit-21','planned')
]

